# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 has "owns_labour"; locke-labour.logic.r1.2 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows; locke-labour.logic.r1.3 has "owns_person → owns_labour"
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "every man has a property in his own person, to which nobody has any right but himself" is true and "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his" is false. Is S2, "The labour of his body, and the work of his hands, we may say, are properly his.", true then?
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3 have "removes → mixes"; locke-labour.logic.r1.2 has "removes → mixes" and "mixes ∧ excludes → property"; they differ when only "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own" and "the labour annexed to the thing excludes the common right of other men to it" are true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own" and "the labour annexed to the thing excludes the common right of other men to it" are true and "he makes the thing his property" is false. Is S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property.", true then?
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 has "owns_labour ∧ mixes ∧ enough_left → excludes"; locke-labour.logic.r1.2 has "owns_labour ∧ removes ∧ mixes ∧ enough_left → excludes"; they differ when only "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own" and "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" are true, which the second allows; locke-labour.logic.r1.3 has "removes ∧ owns_labour ∧ enough_left → excludes" and "excludes → property"
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own" and "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" are true and "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in", "the labour annexed to the thing excludes the common right of other men to it" and "he makes the thing his property" are false. Is S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others.", true then?
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 has "excludes → property"; locke-labour.logic.r1.2 has "owns_person → owns_labour" and "enough_left"; they differ when only "every man has a property in his own person, to which nobody has any right but himself", "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "the labour annexed to the thing excludes the common right of other men to it" and "he makes the thing his property" are true, which the first allows; locke-labour.logic.r1.3 has "removes" and "enough_left"
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.2 have "removes → property"; locke-labour.logic.r1.3 has "property"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered yes, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" and "he makes the thing his property" are false. Is the conclusion, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property.", true then?
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if the labour annexed to the thing excludes the common right of other men to it, then he makes the thing his property"; locke-labour.logic.r1.2 does not
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the labour annexed to the thing excludes the common right of other men to it, then he makes the thing his property"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: locke-labour.logic.r1.2 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3 take for granted "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others"; locke-labour.logic.r1 does not
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/readings: U3: locke-labour.logic.r1.3 takes for granted "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in"; locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.2 do not
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/answered: U3: answered yes, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
locke-labour.md:1: argue/unfaithful: locke-labour.logic.r1.3 P2 (S2): the sentence has no word of "every man has a property in his own person, to which nobody has any right but himself": is it the sentence P2 came from?
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/verdicts: valid as locke-labour.logic.r1.2, locke-labour.logic.r1.3 read it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict
